      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">(Continued on page 14)</h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6;">Laurels in Moot Court Competitions</p>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6;">K.R. Ramamani Taxation Moot Court</p>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6;">A team of students from School of Law, SASTRA comprising K.R. Ramamani, S.N. Bose, S.R. Sharma, S.N. Bose, Kuman M of IV year B.A.,LL.B. (Hons.) and Chi. Amogh S of III year B.A.,LL.B. (Hons.) won the prestigious 6th K.R. Ramamani Memorial Taxation Moot Court Competition 2016 organized by School of Excellence in Law, SASTRA on March 4-6, 2016. They won against VKM College of Law, Chennai and various other schools in the semifinals. The final was judged by Hon'ble Justice Shri P.Bhakmavar of the Madras High Court, Additional Advocate General Shri Arvind Pandiyan and Registrar cum VC in-charge of TNLS. The moot problem was based on international taxation and the competition witnessed participation from various law schools at the national level. Kuman M. Harini S also won the Best Speaker award.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image.jpg" alt="Event Image" style="width: 400px; height: auto;">
        <p style="margin-top: 10px;">N. Natarajan Charitable Trust's Moot Court</p>
      </div>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 20px;">School of Law, SASTRA won the N. Natarajan Charitable Trust's Moot Court Competition organized by the Madras Bar Association from January 29-31, 2016. The team comprising Shri K.R. Ramamani, S.N. Bose, Kuman M of IV year B.A.,LL.B. (Hons.) and Chi. Amogh S of III year B.A.,LL.B. (Hons.) from SASTRA beat the team from the School of Excellence, TN Dr Ambedkar Law University in the finals of the competition. The team was awarded with a rolling trophy and a cash prize of Rs. 45,000/-. The moot problem was related to section under IPC 124 A - Sedition and on Articles 14 (Right to Equality), 19 (Right to Freedom of Expression) and 21 (Protection of Life and Personal Liberty) of the Indian Constitution. Over 150 law schools in Tamil Nadu participated in the moot court competition. The final was judged by the judges of High Court of Madras, namely Justice Shri N.K. Bhukaran, Justice Shri T. Raja and Justice Shri T Sudhanthiram. Shri K.R. Ramamani, S.N. Bose, Kuman M and Chi. Amogh S won the Second Best Speaker Award. The award carried a trophy and a cash prize of Rs. 10,000/-.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column">
    <div>
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Criminal Trial Advocacy National Moot Court</h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6;">The School of Law, SASTRA won the Criminal Trial Advocacy National Moot Court competition held in Bengaluru on February 21, 2016. The moot problem was on the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act. Around 24 teams from across India participated, which included 7 National Law Schools, Symbiosis Pune, ILS Pune, GLC Mumbai & Seoul Chennai. SASTRA beat ILS Pune in the semifinals before convincingly overcoming Amity University Law School in the finals. The team comprising Karthik Subramanian, Shri Ramakrishnan and Chi. Amogh S won the trophy and cash award of Rs.20,000 from Shri Rama Jois, Former Chief Justice of Punjab & Haryana High Court.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image.jpg" alt="Event Image" style="width: 400px; height: auto;">
        <p style="margin-top: 10px;">KNOWLEDGE LEADERSHIP FORUM LECTURES</p>
      </div>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 20px;">Justice Shri S.Nagamuthu, Judge of the Madras High Court delivered a Knowledge Leadership Forum lecture at the School of Law on 'Principles of Criminal Jurisprudence' on February 6, 2016. Delivering the lecture he said criminal law cannot be expressive in all provisions and the judicial discretion must be exercised in the spirit of the criminal jurisprudence in addition to also accepting explainable human nature that aligns with natural human conduct which no law can change.</p>
      <p style="line-height: 1.6;">Justice Nagamuthu covered the various elements and dimensions of the criminal justice administration in India. Dividing India's history of criminal jurisprudence into pre-constitutional and post-constitutional timelines, he said that the rule of law was the basis of administering justice in the country. He stressed on the salience of constitutional principles.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

